{"title":"Function and graphics","markdown":{"headingText":"Function and graphics","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-09_function_viz","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n## Concepts and intuitions {.unnumbered}\nIn ancient times, people have been trying to solve alegraic equations.\nE.g. what are the roots of a second degree polynomial.\nThe famous delta and b2 - 4 a c and the rest.\n\nIn another word, they have been the geometry.\nLooking at figure, let's build a bridge, what is the arch?\n\nEventually, they realize that it is one of the same thing.\nThat is a figure could be represented by a mathematical function.\nAnd mathematical function could be replaced by a figure.\n\nThe ability to plot a function is a blesssing, as long as you can read a figure but picture this.\nWhat if I say your monthly salary is f(year) = 10 exp(year)/ year ^2\nWould you take it?\nBut if we can plot it, I am sure you will take or deny the deal looking at your salary through time.\n\n## Formal {.unnumbered}\nFor simplicity, we look at X and Y as real number.\nSee for more details.\nTo graphically present a function, we need a few things\nx axis to represent the value, we need a scale on that x axis.\ny axis to represent the\nThe angle between x and y axis\nThe curve is just how y vary as we continuously change x\n\n## Examples {.unnumbered}\nThe simplest is y = a*x + b and\nto plot say a second degree polynomial, we could pick n values and plot each of them.\nWell, this means that you can give any function and I can plot it.\nWith computer we can plot anything.\n\n## Tips and warnings {.unnumbered}\nInterpreting figure could be the challenge.\n\n## If you want to know more\nImplicit function\nparameteric plot","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"fre